INSERT OR IGNORE INTO vehicles (slug, name, system, vehicle_class, crew, passengers, speed_ground, speed_air, speed_water, dimensions, weight_tons, mdc_main_body, is_mega_damage, ar, cost, cost_note, description, source_book) VALUES
  ('ng-m56-multibot-original', 'Multibot NG-M56 "Original"', 'rifts', 'robot', 'Two: a pilot and a copilot; the rail gun on top of the flying section needs a gunner of its own', 'Six', 'Running 60 mph (96 km) maximum, cruising at 35 mph (56 km), and running does not tire the operator; handles most ground but is poor on climbs and mountains. Leaping 10 ft (3 m) high or across from a standstill, 20 ft (6 m) high or 30 ft (9 m) across with a running start', 'Flying section only: 240 mph (384 km), ceiling 10,000 ft (3,048 m), +4 to dodge while flying; hovers in place or about 5 ft (1.5 m) off the ground; VTOL. The walking body stays where it was left until the flyer docks again', 'Fair: walks lake and sea floors at half its land speed, and the hover craft skims the surface at up to 120 mph (192 km, 104 knots); maximum depth 1,500 ft (457.2 m)', 'Height 26 ft (7.9 m) with the missile arms folded down, 34 ft (10.4 m) with them raised; width 20 ft (6.1 m); length 19 ft (5.8 m)', '30, fully loaded; 24 without missiles, 20 without the missile arms', 290, 1, NULL, 18000000, 'List price was 34 million credits; a new, undamaged, fully armed unit with missiles can still be had for 18-22 million, and used ones for as little as 10 million. Easy to find in Upper Michigan, Kingsdale, MercTown and Arzno, and wherever soldiers and adventurers do business.', 'Model Type: NG-M56. Class: Modular Ground Assault Robot Vehicle. An older Northern Gun design and, on its release in 100 P.A., the company''s boldest and oddest: a general-purpose machine meant for light combat and exploration alike. Its main guns are an ion cannon in the midsection that swings up and forward when wanted, and two scissor-shaped launcher arms on the back that lift into a V to fire and fold away behind it otherwise; flip-out shoulder shields hide mini-missile launchers, and a gunner''s hatch on top carries an NG-101 rail gun. The upper body, shaped like a head, is itself a hover vehicle. It breaks away instantly (the V-launchers must be folded first, and every missile system stays behind) but takes 1D4 minutes to dock again; once loose it keeps only the ion cannon, pointing straight ahead, and the hatch rail gun, and the walking body it leaves is defenseless unless a guard or another robot stays with it. The idea was a hover car and a robot in one: the flyer could scout, take trails too narrow for the walker, enter towns that ban robots, or run for it. Buyers never warmed to it, put off by thin armor and a strange look (one designer said it lacked sex appeal), and the line closed in 107 P.A. Sensor turret: losing it removes all optical enhancement and sensors, leaving the pilot on his own senses with no bonuses to strike, parry or dodge. Main body damage: once the armature has lost 100 M.D.C., split further main body damage between it and the hover craft, both large targets; the hover craft with no M.D.C. left cannot fly but still runs the robot from inside until the reinforced compartment reaches zero, and then everything shuts down. Robot P.S. 40. Cargo: room for the pilot''s own things and a weapon bin with room for four rifles and pistols, four armor suits, canteens and four weeks of rations; the cockpit also has a small refrigerator and a water cooler holding 100 gallons (378.5 liters). Power: nuclear, 18 year life. Hand to hand per Heavy Ground Robot Training (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.352) at Robot P.S. 40; it cannot make a Restrained Punch, Power Punch or Leap Kick. Sensors: the basic robot set with advanced radar tracking up to 96 targets at once out to 100 miles (160 km).', 'Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One p.154-155'),
  ('ng-m57-2n1-megabot', 'Megabot NG-M57-2N1 (Multibot reboot)', 'rifts', 'robot', 'Two: a pilot and a gunner in a forward cockpit ahead of the passenger section, flying on instruments and video behind a sliding armored cover (120 M.D.C.)', 'Six seated, nine if cramped with some kneeling or on the floor; may be extra crew such as a laser gunner or communications specialist, troops, adventurers or short-term riders', 'Running 55 mph (88 km) maximum, cruising at 35 mph (56 km); suited to most terrain. Leaping is clumsy: 10 ft (3 m) high or across from a standstill, with a run-up, 20 ft (6.1 m) high and across', 'Combat racer only, which cannot truly fly: it rockets along the ground at 250 mph (400 km) with a 500 ft (152 m) ceiling, hovers in place or as low as 4 ft (1.2 m), turns 360 degrees while hovering, VTOL. The robot body stays immobile until the racer comes back', 'With the racer docked the whole robot walks lake and sea floors at 20 mph (32 km) down to 2,000 ft (610 m). The racer alone cannot go under water; it hovers over water up to 60 ft (18.3 m) deep at up to 120 mph (192 km, 104 knots) and sinks in anything deeper', 'Height 37 ft (11.3 m); width 24 ft (7.3 m); length 27 ft (8.2 m) with the racer locked diagonally, nose down. Combat racer alone: 16 ft (4.9 m) from belly to the top of the laser cannon, plus 3.6 ft (1.1 m) on its concealed wheeled landing gear when parked; width 12 ft (3.6 m); length 30 ft (9.1 m)', '51, fully loaded; the combat racer alone 16.3', 375, 1, NULL, 36000000, '42 million credits for robot and racer together with nuclear power and every missile loaded; 36 million with a Solid Oxide Fuel Cell; electric batteries cannot be used. The lowest is stored. The combat racer sells alone, as a vehicle in its own right, for 14 million nuclear or 9 million with a fuel cell.', 'Model Type: NG-M57-2N1 Megabot. Class: Modular Multi-Piece Ground Assault Robot Vehicle. The Multibot never sold, but engineers and Operators in Ishpeming loved it, and their enthusiasm plus political pressure for jobs reopened its assembly line with this updated machine, launched with NG''s new catalog in spring 109 P.A. as a battlefield command robot, VIP escort and exploration vehicle. Unlike its forerunner it looks like serious military hardware: heavy armor, mini-, short- and medium-range missiles and a three-barreled laser, and it is among the largest robots NG builds. The upper body is a hover combat racer that breaks away with the whole crew and passengers for speed, scouting, quick strikes or escape, carrying a heavy ion cannon in the underside of its nose. Critics name four faults the Multibot shared and one new one: the racer gets in the way in a brawl, makes a tempting target, gives big foes a handhold and leaves a blind spot under the belly; its weight up front makes the robot top heavy (+10% to knock it down with a body block or ram); the body, limbs and launchers it leaves behind are immobile and unguarded; if the racer dies while away the body is useless and easy pickings; and docked, the ion cannon can only fire 45 degrees down at the ground ahead, firing forward only once the racer is loose (the torso turns 180 degrees at the waist to aim it). Sales still run three to one over the Multibot, popular with speed lovers, robot aces and special forces; it should be escorted by smaller robots, vehicles, power armor or troops who can guard the body while the racer is away. Uses: parking the body somewhere safe while the racer scouts, runs supplies or reaches towns and dig sites as fast as a hovercycle with all six aboard; or pounding a foe with missiles, then detaching to charge, outflank or withdraw; the racer alone does search and rescue, border patrol, messages, insertions and extractions, medevac, rapid response, and hunts down hovercycles, ground vehicles and power armor flying low. Sensors: losing either the racer sensor sphere or the shoulder sensor/communications array does nothing; losing both removes all optics and sensors and every sensor bonus to strike, parry and dodge. Windows: the passenger plexiglass (20 M.D.C. each) hides behind sliding alloy plates (50 more each) at a button or voice command; the cockpit glass only matters once the cameras and sensors are out or the one-piece pop-off cover (120 M.D.C.) is shot away and ejected. Cameras sit in the cover (two), the sensor mast over the left shoulder, and hidden in the nose and tail. With both window and plate gone, the reinforced compartment is exposed, and a passenger can be targeted by a Called Shot at -4. Fins: one lost costs the racer -2% speed and -2% piloting; both, -5% of each. Main thrusters: one lost halves speed and dodge and costs -20% piloting; both leave it at 30 mph (48 km), 30 ft (9.1 m) up at most, with no dodge bonus and -40% on evasive or trick maneuvers. Combat racer statistical data (the one vehicles row holds both pieces): speed, water and size as stated in those fields; weight 16.3 tons; no P.S. of its own; power nuclear, 20 years for the racer on its own, or Solid Oxide Fuel Cell, never batteries, and it carries the whole robot''s power supply, so the body is dead without it; seating for six plus a closet-sized weapons locker, a tiny airliner-style toilet and washroom, under-seat and overhead stowage, a small refrigerator and a water cooler holding 120 gallons (454 liters); the cockpit holds the pilot''s things and a weapon bin for four rifles and pistols, a pair of Vibro-Knives (1D4 M.D.), a pair of armor suits, four canteens and food for two people for a week. The racer''s nose bends where it docks; sold alone it is usually braced rigid, and the braces come off to fit a Megabot body. Robot P.S. 40. Power: nuclear, 15 year life for the full robot; Solid Oxide Fuel Cell available. Hand to hand per Heavy Ground Robot Training (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.352), with no Power Punch or Leap Kick. Features beyond the NG standard: cameras for driving and navigation feeding the cockpit and passengers'' helmet HUDs, relayed to monitors and to designated receivers within 500 miles (800 km); each camera reaches 4,000 ft (1,219 m) with telescopic and x10 macro lenses and stores 10,000 hours of video.', 'Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One p.157-160'),
  ('ng-exc17-ogre', 'Ogre EXC-17 Explorer & Construction', 'rifts', 'robot', 'Two: a pilot and a copilot/communications engineer or Operator; the pilot can run every function and weapon alone if he must', 'Three seated in the cockpit; living compartments for four, or 8-12 crammed in, sleeping on floors and cockpit seats', 'Running 35 mph (56 km) top speed; it cannot leap. Very hard to knock down: only attackers of similar size and power can try, at a third of the usual chance; anything smaller or under 12 tons cannot, and a small rammer does half damage to it and takes the same itself', 'Not possible', 'Cannot swim; walks the bottom at 20 mph (32 km); maximum depth 3,000 ft (914 m)', 'Height 33 ft (10 m); width 35 ft (10.6 m); body length 30 ft (9.1 m) to the nose, the ion cannon adding 4.5 ft (1.4 m); the arms hang to the ground like a gorilla''s when slack, reaching 30 ft (9.1 m) right and 36 ft (11 m) left', '42', 570, 1, NULL, 28000000, '35 million credits nuclear (this line prints a 15 year life, the power system line 20), 28 million with a Solid Oxide Fuel Cell; no electric battery option. The lowest is stored. Fair to good availability.', 'Model Type: NG-EXC-17 Ogre. Class: Exploration and Construction Robot Vehicle. Designed by Frederick R. Seibel the 5th, Ammon Redman and Ramzie Othman, and named for its squat, hunched, heavy-handed build: it looks ready to pounce even standing still. A heavy labor machine that doubles as a wilderness explorer and camper, trading speed and guns for strength, armor, versatile hands and living space: four sleeper compartments, a walk-in-closet storage bay, a small shower and toilet and plenty of water. The right hand is the nimbler one, able to pick up and study specimens as well as haul cargo and lumber, uproot trees and crush stone; a camera and sensor pod on it records work and wildlife and scans ore, sites and wreckage, and slides along a track or retracts out of harm''s way. The left hand lifts trees and debris, digs, mines, drills and excavates; its fingers lock and the whole hand spins at the wrist to fell trees, bore into earth, rock or M.D.C. fortifications, cut trenches and foundations, and wreck walls. The Ogre lifts five times and drags twelve times what its strength class normally allows (P.S. 45), and serves for construction, hauling, field repair, robot recovery, salvage and cutting up wrecks. The nose cockpit seats five behind seven windows with a fine view, though riders are a liability in a fight; the whole upper torso turns 360 degrees. It can wrestle dinosaurs, monsters and robots up to 20% bigger than itself, and its wedge shape, short legs and broad feet make it nearly impossible to knock over. Its weak points are the windows and a slow 35 mph top speed, a deliberate choice for a work and survey machine. It fords fast rivers, wades lakes, stands up to mudslides, floods, herds and big predators, and handles snow, ice and drifts almost unslowed, so it is often sent to dig out robots and vehicles stuck in northern winters. Sensors: destroying both sensor arrays (nose and hand) knocks out radar, targeting and optics, leaving the pilot on his own senses with no sensor bonuses. Windows: the plexiglass (15 M.D.C. each) is covered in combat by sliding alloy plates (50 more each) at a button or voice command, and the pilot then drives on the nose or hand cameras and instruments; a Called Shot at -3 targets a window, and with glass and plate gone the reinforced compartment is open, then the crew. Robot P.S. 45. Cargo: the living quarters'' trunk and two footlockers, a cargo closet 8 by 4 by 8 ft (2.4 by 1.2 by 2.4 m), a refrigerator-sized specimen cabinet, and an overhead luggage bin at each cockpit seat for a rifle, backpack and basic gear. Power: nuclear, 20 year average life; Solid Oxide Fuel Cell available. Hand to hand per Heavy Ground Robot Training (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.352), except: restrained punch 1D6 M.D.; a punch, tear or crush at full strength 3D6+5 M.D. right hand, 3D6+9 M.D. left; power punch 1D6x10+7 M.D. right, 1D6x10+12 M.D. left; left-hand spinning drill strike 1D6x10+30 M.D.; stomps for 3D6 M.D. against targets under 8 ft (2.4 m) tall; ram/body block standard; no head butt, leaps or kicks. Power punches and the spinning strike count as two attacks. Features beyond the NG standard: right hand camera and sensor pod with an infrared and visible light beam (1,000 ft/305 m), a video camera feeding the crew compartment and the screens over the bunks (6,000 ft/1,829 m, telescopic and x10 macro lenses, 10,000 hours of storage, transmission), and the standard sensor suite plus motion detection (100 ft/30.5 m); passive nightvision for pilot, gunner and communications officer (2,000 ft/610 m, useless in total darkness); a translator holding the Americas'' nine known languages, with room for 12 more, following up to three voices and two languages, 98.7% accurate with a three second delay for one speaker, 78% with six seconds for three; and two winches with hook and tow line, one at the back and one hidden in the left hand, for hauling, towing robots, vehicles and barges, and climbing.', 'Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One p.160-163'),
  ('ng-ex11-okemos', 'Okemos Explorer', 'rifts', 'robot', 'One pilot; the turret''s open-air seat takes a gunner/communications engineer', 'Three', 'Running 72 mph (115.2 km) maximum, cruising at 30-50 mph (48-80 km) as terrain and weather allow. Leaping 20 ft (6.1 m) high and 30 ft (9.1 m) across, standing or running', 'Not possible', 'Not built for it; walks the bottom at 30 mph (48 km); maximum depth 800 ft (244 m)', 'Height 20 ft (6.1 m) from cockpit top to toe, 26 ft (7.9 m) with the big gun''s 6 ft (1.8 m); width at the hips 12 ft (3.6 m); length 17.6 ft (5.4 m)', '7.5', 295, 1, NULL, 11000000, '18 million credits nuclear, 12 million with a Solid Oxide Fuel Cell, 11 million with an electric battery; the lowest is stored. The turret and nose weapon options are priced separately in the weapon notes.', 'Model Type: EX-11. Nicknames: Little Chief and NG Scout ''Bot. Class: Exploration Robot, suited to scouting, travel, long-range recon, wilderness exploration and light combat. From NG''s Department of Exploratory Robot Designs, by the Redapple Design Team led by Peter Adams, Chris Axtell and David Snell, who set out to build a small, quick, adaptable and affordable machine for adventurers and explorers. Federico Franceschi, head of NG Research and Development and a student of Michigan''s history, named it after John Okemos, a Saginaw Chippewa chief whose name (Ogimaans in Ojibwa) means Little Chief, and whose namesake town near Lansing was once an industrial center. A light, four-person, two-legged walker made for long trips across hostile country: it steps over logs, boulders and slides, pushes through tall grass, brush and thin woods, and wades swamps and streams. Two hydraulic arms reach 12 ft (3.6 m) to gather specimens or supplies, climb, repair larger machines and brawl a little. The pilot sits in a forward cab under a big M.D.C. bubble, which buyers insisted on so they could see the country; a 100 M.D.C. plate with a narrow slit can be bolted over it in 1D6+6 minutes with tools. The glass is printed as 35 M.D.C. twice in the text and 40 in the location block, which is stored; hitting it takes a Called Shot at -2, and with the glass (and any plate) gone the pilot has only whatever body armor he wears (usually 28-100 M.D.C.) or a light suit of power armor, a Called Shot at -3. Two spotlights flank the top of the cab and swivel 30 degrees every way; each shoulder has a smaller light (3 M.D.C.) and a digital camera (1 M.D.C.). The thing in the nose that looks like a small gun is a recorder: 8,000 hours of video with sound, 12x telescopic zoom to 6,000 ft (1,828 m) and a 30x macro down to 3mm; another camera sits in the main turret, and every feed goes to the pilot''s HUD and the monitors in cockpit, turret and berths. Its best travel feature is four sealed private berths, each with a bed that folds into a recliner, a light and screen overhead, a steel sink with 100 gallons (378.5 liters) of wash water, a mirror and medicine cabinet, 50 gallons (189.2 liters) of drinking water with purification for doubtful sources, two fold-out tables, a trunk at the foot that doubles as a seat, a clothes rack, and a flushless toilet that vaporizes waste with a light S.D.C. ion system (the smell hangs on for 3D6 minutes); each has two windows (15 M.D.C.) with sliding 40 M.D.C. plates. Arms are limited to a nose turret and a top turret above the berths that also houses a second major camera, plus a round communications and sensor array with a spotlight, long-range radio and laser relay. The turret''s open gunner seat is quick to get into and out of, and the turret, armored panel and gun shield the gunner so well that he can only be hit from the side at an angle, by a Called Shot at -6. It is nose-heavy and bobs as it walks (riders compare it to a camel, then get used to it), and it is sturdy, reliable and popular with adventurers, explorers, mercenaries and merchants who tear out the beds to haul cargo in the berths. Robot P.S. 30. Cargo: minimal; the 3x3x3 ft (0.9 m) trunk in each berth, hangers, and room for a rifle, a pistol, a survival kit, a few backpacks and personal effects. Power: nuclear, 20 year average life; fuel cell and battery versions are sold too. Hand to hand per Light Ground Robot Training (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.353) at Robot P.S. 30 with bonuses halved, rounded down; it is not built to fight. Sensors: the robot standard plus long-range radio and four cameras (nose, main gun and one in each shoulder), shock-proofed by layered acoustic damping, with lenses that turn to gel on impact and are reset hard by a small current afterwards.', 'Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One p.163-166');

